1. INTRODUCTION 
 
Since Narzt et al. [1] proposed an outlook of applying augmented reality (AR) technologies into the automotive head-up display (HUD), interactive AR-HUD systems have become available [2], [3] thanks to the development of related sensing and display technologies. With information of high-precision geographic positioning, vehicle orientation estimation, and other internal or external signals, such a HUD can visualize virtual objects, e.g. speed limit signs, turning arrows, and street names as if they were merged with the real scenes [4]. Thus, a driver can receive necessary information about navigation, driving conditions, and other infotainment content without being distracted by a dashboard or video screen. 
